    },
    {
      "text": "Building a Real-Time Collaboration Engine from Scratch",
      "fontFamily": "Geist",
      "fontSize": 42,
      "fontWeight": "700",
      "color": "#EDEDF0",
      "lineHeight": 1.2
    },
    {
      "text": "A deep dive into CRDTs, operational transforms, and the architecture decisions behind conflict-free real-time editing.",
      "fontFamily": "Inter",
      "fontSize": 18,
      "fontWeight": "400",
      "color": "#8B8B96",
      "lineHeight": 1.5
    },
    {
      "text": "Jul 15, 2026",
      "fontFamily": "Geist Mono",
      "fontSize": 13,
      "fontWeight": "400",
      "color": "#5C5C66"
    },
    {
      "text": "Real-time collaboration is one of those features that seems deceptively simple from the outside. Users type, and everyone sees the changes. But beneath the surface lies a fascinating set of distributed systems challenges that have occupied computer scientists for decades.",
      "fontFamily": "Inter",
      "fontSize": 17,
      "fontWeight": "400",
      "color": "#8B8B96",
      "lineHeight": 1.8
    },
    {
      "text": "The Problem with Naive Approaches",
      "fontFamily": "Geist",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontWeight": "700",
      "color": "#EDEDF0"
    },
    {
      "text": "The simplest approach — last-write-wins — breaks down immediately in a collaborative context. When two users edit the same paragraph simultaneously, one person's changes will be silently overwritten. This is not just a bad user experience; it's data loss.",
      "fontFamily": "Inter",
      "fontSize": 17,
      "fontWeight": "400",
      "color": "#8B8B96",
      "lineHeight": 1.8
    },
    {
      "text": "Operational Transform (OT) was the first serious attempt to solve this. Google Docs uses OT, and it works well — but it requires a central server to serialize operations. For our use case, we wanted something that could work peer-to-peer.",
      "fontFamily": "Inter",
      "fontSize": 17,
      "fontWeight": "400",
      "color": "#8B8B96",
      "lineHeight": 1.8
    },
    {
      "text": "\"CRDTs guarantee that if two replicas have received the same set of updates — possibly in different orders — they will be in the same state. No coordination required.\"",
      "fontFamily": "Inter",
      "fontSize": 17,
      "fontWeight": "400",
      "color": "#8B8B96",
      "lineHeight": 1.8
    },
    {
      "text": "Enter CRDTs",
      "fontFamily": "Geist",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontWeight": "700",
      "color": "#EDEDF0"
    },
    {
      "text": "Conflict-free Replicated Data Types solve this elegantly. Instead of transforming operations against each other, CRDTs define data structures where all concurrent operations commute — meaning the order doesn't matter. The result is always the same.",
      "fontFamily": "Inter",
      "fontSize": 17,
      "fontWeight": "400",
      "color": "#8B8B96",
      "lineHeight": 1.8
    },
    {
      "text": "typescript",
      "fontFamily": "Geist Mono",
      "fontSize": 11,
      "fontWeight": "600",
      "color": "#5C5C66"
    },
    {
      "text": "interface CRDT<T> {",
      "fontFamily": "Geist Mono",
      "fontSize": 13,
      "fontWeight": "400",
      "color": "#607EBC",
      "lineHeight": 1.6
    },
    {
      "text": "state: T;",
      "fontFamily": "Geist Mono",
      "fontSize": 13,
      "fontWeight": "400",
      "color": "#8B8B96",
      "lineHeight": 1.6
    },
    {
      "text": "merge(remote: T): T;",
      "fontFamily": "Geist Mono",
      "fontSize": 13,
      "fontWeight": "400",
      "color": "#8B8B96",
      "lineHeight": 1.6
    },
    {
      "text": "apply(op: Operation): T;",
      "fontFamily": "Geist Mono",
      "fontSize": 13,
      "fontWeight": "400",
      "color": "#8B8B96",
      "lineHeight": 1.6
    },
    {
      "text": "class TextCRDT implements CRDT<Doc> {",
      "fontFamily": "Geist Mono",
      "fontSize": 13,
      "fontWeight": "400",
      "color": "#607EBC",
      "lineHeight": 1.6
    },
    {
      "text": "merge(remote: Doc): Doc {",
      "fontFamily": "Geist Mono",
      "fontSize": 13,
      "fontWeight": "400",
      "color": "#8B8B96",
      "lineHeight": 1.6
    },
    {
      "text": "return this.state.integrate(remote);",
      "fontFamily": "Geist Mono",
      "fontSize": 13,
      "fontWeight": "400",
      "color": "#8B8B96",
      "lineHeight": 1.6
    },
    {
      "text": "Architecture Decisions",
      "fontFamily": "Geist",
      "fontSize": 22,
      "fontWeight": "600",
      "color": "#EDEDF0"
    },
    {
      "text": "We chose Yjs as our CRDT library for its maturity and performance characteristics. The architecture consists of three layers: a thin client SDK that wraps Yjs, a WebSocket relay server for propagating updates, and an optional persistence layer backed by PostgreSQL.",
      "fontFamily": "Inter",
      "fontSize": 17,
      "fontWeight": "400",
      "color": "#8B8B96",
      "lineHeight": 1.8
    },
    {
      "text": "The relay server is intentionally stateless — it simply broadcasts updates to all connected clients in a room. This means horizontal scaling is trivial: spin up more relay servers behind a load balancer and use Redis Pub/Sub for cross-server communication.",
      "fontFamily": "Inter",
      "fontSize": 17,
      "fontWeight": "400",
      "color": "#8B8B96",
      "lineHeight": 1.8
    },
    {
      "text": "Results & Lessons Learned",
      "fontFamily": "Geist",
      "fontSize": 22,
      "fontWeight": "600",
      "color": "#EDEDF0"
    },
    {
      "text": "After six months in production, the system handles 500+ concurrent editors with sub-50ms sync latency. The key takeaway: CRDTs aren't magic, but they dramatically simplify the mental model. Instead of reasoning about operation ordering, you reason about data structure properties.",
      "fontFamily": "Inter",
      "fontSize": 17,
      "fontWeight": "400",
      "color": "#8B8B96",
      "lineHeight": 1.8
    },
